<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pointe-à-Pitre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pointe-à-Pitre is the largest town and main commercial port of Guadeloupe, on the island of Grande-Terre at the southern end of the channel separating it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basse-terre">Basse-Terre</a>. Developed from the 1760s, it grew into the economic centre of the colony, though the town of Basse-Terre remained the seat of government. It has been rebuilt several times after earthquakes, fires and hurricanes, notably the earthquake of 1843.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pointe-a-pitre.jpg" alt="Pointe-à-Pitre" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zivax · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pointe-%C3%A0-Pitre.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pointe-à-Pitre stands on the southwest of Grande-Terre, facing the Caribbean Sea, near the Rivière Salée, the Salt River that separates Grande-Terre from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basse-terre">Basse-Terre</a>. It lies on a limestone plateau, which was a factor in the city's construction, and its bay, the Petit Cul-de-Sac Marin, offers a sheltered port. The name means headland of Pitre. It is popularly derived from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sailor or fisherman called Pieter who may have settled on a promontory in the 17th century, but linguists now question this and suggest the Spanish word pitera, a rope made from agave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>French colonial authorities had long thought of founding a city where Guadeloupe's two main districts meet, but attempts around 1713 to 1730 failed because the swampy ground was unhealthy. During the British occupation of Guadeloupe from 1759 to 1763 a settlement appeared on a hill overlooking the swamps. After Guadeloupe returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1763 the city was officially founded in 1764 by royal edict under the governor Gabriel de Clieu, and the swamps were drained in the following years, which allowed urban development.</p><p>Growth was relatively rapid, partly thanks to the corsairs. A great fire entirely destroyed the city in 1780, and an earthquake destroyed it again in 1843. Its history is marked by many disasters: fires in 1850, 1871 and 1931, earthquakes in 1851 and 1897, hurricanes in 1865 and 1928, and several cholera epidemics. After the 1928 hurricane the architect Ali Tur built much of the city's civil and religious heritage between 1930 and 1935, including the palais de justice, the hospice hospital, the fish hall, several schools and the fire station. Its location and large sheltered port nevertheless made it Guadeloupe's largest city and economic capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pointe-à-Pitre is the second most populous commune of Guadeloupe after Les Abymes, and an economic capital though not the administrative capital, which is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basse-terre">Basse-Terre</a>. The commune itself is small, but it is the centre of an urban area of eleven communes holding about 65 percent of the island's population. The main seaport, Port de Jarry, lies across the bay in Baie-Mahault. The former cathedral of St Peter and Paul recalls that the town was the seat of a Roman Catholic diocese, united with Basse-Terre in 1951. Its monuments include the Place de la Victoire, the Schœlcher and Saint-John-Perse museums and the Mémorial ACTe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pointe-à-Pitre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A royal governor ruled from Basse-Terre, and <b>Pointe-à-Pitre</b> grew into the main commercial port.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-guadeloupe">Colony of Guadeloupe</a></li></ul></section>
